---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO_URL } from '../../lib/release';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const href = (p: string) => (base.endsWith('/') ? base : base + '/') + p.replace(/^\//, '');

/** Drives the nav rail and every heading anchor, so the two cannot drift. */
const steps = [
  { id: 'draw', label: 'Draw a territory' },
  { id: 'scan', label: 'Find the leads' },
  { id: 'sheet', label: 'Read the call sheet' },
  { id: 'phones', label: 'Work the phones' },
  { id: 'emails', label: 'Find emails' },
  { id: 'settings', label: 'Settings' },
  { id: 'rescan', label: 'Rescanning' },
  { id: 'trouble', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
  { id: 'calling', label: 'Calling responsibly' },
];
---

<Base
  title="How to use Quadrant"
  description="From installing Quadrant to working a call sheet: drawing a territory, scanning it, reading the ranking, and tracking calls."
>
  <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom:36px">
    <div class="wrap doc">
      <a class="backlink" href={href("")}>Back to home</a>
      <h1>How to use Quadrant</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="max-width:66ch">
        From a blank map to a call sheet you can work through. About ten minutes
        end to end, most of it waiting for the first scan.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap doc">
    <div class="doc-grid">
      <nav class="doc-nav" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="doc-nav-title">On this page</p>
        <ol>
          {steps.map((s) => (
            <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a></li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="doc-body">
        <section id="draw">
          <p class="eyebrow">Step one</p>
          <h2>Draw a territory</h2>

          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              A <strong>box</strong> is a named area that owns its own leads, call
              statuses and notes. Most people keep one per niche and city &mdash;
              <em>Scottsdale Dentists</em>, <em>Tampa Chiropractors</em>.
            </p>
            <ol class="steps">
              <li>Click <strong>+ New box</strong> in the sidebar.</li>
              <li>
                Press and drag on the map to draw a rectangle. Draw it over a
                <strong>US city</strong> &mdash; the registry Quadrant reads is a US
                federal system, and it will refuse a box drawn anywhere else rather
                than let you scan for nothing.
              </li>
              <li>
                Name it, then tick the specialties you want. The dialog shows how
                many ZIP codes fall inside, which is the best early signal of how
                big the scan will be.
              </li>
            </ol>

            <div class="note">
              <p>
                <strong>Start small.</strong> A few neighbourhood blocks comes back
                in about five seconds. A whole metro area took three minutes and
                returned 762 practices. Draw small first and see whether the list is
                worth calling before you commit to a big one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top:20px">
              Lost on the map? <strong>Show US</strong> in the bottom-right corner
              brings you back. Want more room for the list? Drag the bar under the
              map to resize it, or <strong>Hide map</strong> to reclaim the space
              entirely &mdash; pressing <strong>+ New box</strong> reopens it for you.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="scan">
          <p class="eyebrow">Step two</p>
          <h2>Find the leads</h2>

          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              With the box selected, click <strong>Find leads</strong>. Quadrant
              works out which ZIP codes your rectangle covers, queries the provider
              registry for each one, drops the practices you would never pitch, and
              ranks what remains.
            </p>
            <p>
              Progress shows as it goes, and <strong>Stop</strong> cancels at any
              point without losing what has already been found.
            </p>

            <h3 style="margin-top:26px">What gets dropped, and why</h3>
            <p>
              Hospitals, assisted-living operators, home-health agencies, equipment
              suppliers and pharmacies are filtered out. They have procurement
              departments, not a doctor who picks up the phone. Duplicate
              registrations that share a phone number are merged into one row, so
              you never dial the same front desk twice &mdash; the row tells you how
              many practices sit behind that number.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="sheet">
          <p class="eyebrow">Step three</p>
          <h2>Read the call sheet</h2>

          <div class="tblwrap" style="margin-bottom:22px">
            <table class="tbl">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Column</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Score</strong></td>
                  <td>Call order. Red is hot, amber warm, grey cool. Click any row to see the reasoning.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Practice</strong></td>
                  <td>Who you are calling, and their city. A badge appears when other practices share the number.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Phone</strong></td>
                  <td>The practice line. Click to dial if your machine handles <code>tel:</code> links.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Ask for</strong></td>
                  <td>The named official on the registry record, and their title. This is the difference between a conversation and a gatekeeper.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Their time</strong></td>
                  <td>The clock <em>at the practice</em>, not yours. Green means inside your calling window.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Status</strong></td>
                  <td>Where you are with them. Set it from the row while you are on the call.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="prose">
            <h3>Why a lead scored what it did</h3>
            <p>
              Click a row to expand it. <strong>Why this lead</strong> lists the
              actual signals &mdash; a direct line that bypasses reception, a
              practice registered in the last couple of years, a strong specialty
              fit. A registry record nobody has updated in five years <em>loses</em>
              points and says so.
            </p>
            <p>
              The panel also carries the direct line, address, website, email,
              registration date and the NPI, plus a <strong>Call notes</strong> box
              that saves when you click away.
            </p>

            <h3 style="margin-top:26px">The best signal on the sheet</h3>
            <p>
              A practice registered recently is a <strong>new clinic</strong> &mdash;
              no admin staff hired yet, still setting up how it runs. That is the
              warmest call available to a VA, and it is why score is worth trusting
              as call order rather than sorting alphabetically.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="phones">
          <p class="eyebrow">Step four</p>
          <h2>Work the phones</h2>

          <div class="prose">
            <p>Five statuses, each with a different next action:</p>
            <dl class="kv" style="max-width:52ch">
              <dt><strong>New</strong></dt><dd>Not called yet</dd>
              <dt><strong>No answer</strong></dt><dd>Tried, nobody picked up &mdash; try again later</dd>
              <dt><strong>Follow up</strong></dt><dd>Reached out, waiting on them</dd>
              <dt><strong>Interested</strong></dt><dd>Pursue this one</dd>
              <dt><strong>Do not contact</strong></dt><dd>Stop calling. Honor this the first time you are asked.</dd>
            </dl>

            <h3 style="margin-top:26px">Filters that earn their keep</h3>
            <p>
              <strong>Open now</strong> hides every practice outside your calling
              window, which is the single most useful filter when you are working
              across US timezones from elsewhere. The status filter lets you sweep
              back through everyone marked <em>No answer</em>, and the specialty
              filter lets you batch one niche at a time so your pitch stays sharp.
            </p>
            <p>
              Prefer bigger rows? Switch <strong>List</strong> to
              <strong>Cards</strong>. Same leads, same filters, roomier layout.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="emails">
          <p class="eyebrow">Step five</p>
          <h2>Find emails, if you want them</h2>

          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              <strong>Find emails</strong> is a separate button on purpose. The scan
              is fast; hunting emails is slow &mdash; roughly a second per practice
              &mdash; because it has to find each practice&rsquo;s website and read
              it. Doing it in one pass would make you wait for a call sheet you
              could already be working.
            </p>
            <p>
              It is cancellable and saves as it goes, so stopping halfway keeps
              everything found so far.
            </p>

            <div class="candid">
              <h3>Set your expectations here</h3>
              <p>
                The registry holds <strong>no email addresses at all</strong>. Every
                one Quadrant shows was found by locating a practice&rsquo;s site and
                reading it, and plenty of practices publish a contact form instead
                of an address. Expect roughly a quarter to a third, not
                near-total like phone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h3 style="margin-top:26px">Published versus guessed</h3>
            <p>
              An address shown in plain text was <strong>published</strong> on the
              practice&rsquo;s own site. One shown in amber with a dashed underline
              was <strong>guessed</strong> &mdash; Quadrant confirmed the domain
              accepts mail and inferred <code>info@</code>, which is usually right
              but can bounce.
            </p>
            <p>
              Use the contact filter&rsquo;s <strong>Published email only</strong>
              option before any mail-out. Bounces cost sender reputation, and that
              distinction is exactly what the filter is for. Turn guessing off
              entirely in Settings if you would rather not have them at all.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="settings">
          <p class="eyebrow">Worth setting once</p>
          <h2>Settings</h2>

          <div class="tblwrap">
            <table class="tbl">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Why you would change it</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Appearance</strong></td>
                  <td>Follow system, light or dark. The map follows too.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>When you call</strong></td>
                  <td>
                    Your calling window and days. This is what <strong>Open
                    now</strong> means &mdash; change it from 9&ndash;5 and the
                    filter changes with it. The hours are hours <em>at the
                    practice</em>.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>New boxes start with</strong></td>
                  <td>Which specialties are pre-ticked when you name a box. Set it to your niche and stop re-ticking.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Finding emails</strong></td>
                  <td>How hard the hunt tries, and whether guessed addresses are produced at all.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Your data</strong></td>
                  <td>Where your boxes live on disk, with a button to open that folder.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="rescan">
          <p class="eyebrow">Keeping it current</p>
          <h2>Rescanning does not lose your work</h2>

          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              <strong>Rescan</strong> re-queries the registry for the same box.
              Practices that have appeared since last time get added, and details
              that changed get refreshed &mdash; but your call statuses, your notes
              and any emails already found are kept exactly as they were.
            </p>
            <p>
              Worth doing every few weeks. New registrations are the warmest leads
              on the sheet, and a rescan is how they reach you.
            </p>
            <p class="muted" style="font-size:14px">
              Everything is stored as plain files on your machine, one folder per
              box. There is no cloud copy &mdash; back the folder up if the work in
              it matters.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="trouble">
          <p class="eyebrow">When something looks wrong</p>
          <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

          <div class="grid-2">
            <div class="card">
              <h3>It won&rsquo;t let me create my box</h3>
              <p>
                The box is outside the United States. The registry is a US federal
                system, so a box elsewhere can only ever come back empty &mdash;
                Quadrant blocks it rather than wasting your time. Use <strong>Show
                US</strong> and draw again.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
              <h3>The scan returned nothing</h3>
              <p>
                Either no ZIP codes fall inside the box, or none of the practices
                there match the specialties you ticked. The empty table says which.
                Add specialties, or draw a wider box.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
              <h3>Windows blocked the installer</h3>
              <p>
                The installer is unsigned, so SmartScreen warns about it. Click
                <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>. Verify
                the checksum on the download page first if you would rather check.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
              <h3>A phone number reached the wrong place</h3>
              <p>
                Registry records go stale, and some numbers route to a billing
                service. Expanding the row shows when the record was last updated
                &mdash; an old date is the usual explanation.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="calling">
          <p class="eyebrow">Before you dial</p>
          <h2>Calling responsibly</h2>

          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              The numbers come from a public federal registry. What you do with them
              is governed by rules Quadrant cannot enforce for you: honor the
              National Do Not Call registry wherever a number is a personal line,
              say who you are and who you are calling for, and honor an opt-out the
              first time you are asked.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Do not contact</strong> exists for that last one, and it
              survives every rescan.
            </p>
            <p class="muted" style="font-size:14px">
              None of this is legal advice. If you are calling at volume, check the
              current rules for the states you are calling into.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap doc" style="text-align:center">
      <h2>That&rsquo;s the whole tool.</h2>
      <div class="cta-row" style="justify-content:center">
        <a class="btn primary" href={href('download/')}>Download for Windows</a>
        <a class="btn" href={REPO_URL}>Read the source</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
